WRITE(1)                       P32W Unix                        WRITE(1)

NAME
       write - write to another user

SYNOPSIS
       write user [ttyN]

DESCRIPTION
       write copies what you type to user's terminal, a line at a
       time, until the end of the input (^D) or an interrupt (^C),
       and then writes EOF there. It first rings the bell and
       writes a header there:

              Message from ken on tty0 at 14:05 ...

       (your name, your terminal and the local time). The user is
       looked for in /etc/utmp. If they are logged in on more than
       one terminal and you name none, write uses the first and
       says so; ttyN picks one.

       For a conversation the other user runs write back to you;
       by custom each ends a turn with a line o (over) and the end
       with oo (over and out).

       A user who has said mesg n cannot be written to: write:
       user has messages disabled. This is checked again before
       each line, so mesg n during a conversation ends it. Root
       can write to any terminal.

       Control characters other than tab and bell are shown as ^X
       (DEL as ^?), and bytes over 127 as M- and the character, so
       a message cannot send the other terminal escape sequences.

EXAMPLES
       $ write guest
       lunch at one? o
       ^D

       $ echo 'backup at 18:00' | write ken tty1

FILES
       /etc/utmp      who is logged in where
       /dev/tty0 ...  the terminals: owner the user, group tty,
                      mode 0620 (0600 after mesg n)
       /bin/write     set-group-id tty: mode 2755, owner root,
                      group tty

EXIT STATUS
       0 when the message was written; 1 if the user is unknown,
       not logged in (on that terminal) or has messages disabled,
       or the terminal cannot be written; 2 for a usage error.

SEE ALSO
       mesg(1), wall(1), who(1), mail(1), login(1)

BUGS
       write is set-group-id tty: it opens the terminal with that
       group and then gives the group up (setgid(2) to your own)
       before it reads anything. It never needs root. Without a
       terminal of your own the header says only "from name at"
       the time. The other user's lines are not shown to you
       until they run write themselves.
